### What are the three partitioning strategies?

Kafka provides three ways to decide which partition a record goes to: no key (sticky or round-robin), key-based hashing, and a custom partitioner. Each makes a different trade-off between ordering and load distribution.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;

public class PartitionStrategies {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Strategy 1: No key -- sticky/round-robin
        ProducerRecord<String, String> noKey =
            new ProducerRecord<>("audit-log", null, "{\"event\":\"login\"}");

        // Strategy 2: Key-based -- hash of key
        ProducerRecord<String, String> keyed =
            new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", "SAV2001", "{\"amount\":50000}");

        // Strategy 3: Explicit partition (bypasses partitioner)
        ProducerRecord<String, String> explicit =
            new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", 0, "SAV2001", "{\"amount\":50000}");

        System.out.println("No key   : sticky/round-robin across partitions");
        System.out.println("With key : murmur2(SAV2001) % numPartitions");
        System.out.println("Explicit : forced to partition 0");
    }
}
```

```text
No key   : sticky/round-robin across partitions
With key : murmur2(SAV2001) % numPartitions
Explicit : forced to partition 0
```

### How does the sticky partitioner work (no key)?

When a record has no key, modern Kafka uses the sticky partitioner. It fills one partition for a batch, then switches to another. This keeps batches full and throughput high while still spreading records across partitions over time. The older behavior was strict round-robin per record.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import java.util.Properties;

public class StickyPartitionerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092");
        props.put("key.serializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put("value.serializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put("linger.ms", "10");

        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
                // No key -- sticky partitioner fills current batch
                producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("audit-log", null,
                    "{\"event\":\"page_view\",\"seq\":" + i + "}"));
            }
            System.out.println("100 keyless records sent -- spread across partitions");
            System.out.println("Batch efficiency is high because sticky fills each batch");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
100 keyless records sent -- spread across partitions
Batch efficiency is high because sticky fills each batch
```

### How does hash-of-key partitioning work?

When a key is present, the default partitioner computes `murmur2(keyBytes) % numPartitions`. The same key always maps to the same partition, so all records for that key are ordered. In a bank, you key by account id so a debit written before a credit is consumed before that credit.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.common.utils.Utils;

public class HashPartitionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int numPartitions = 12;
        String[] accounts = {"SAV2001", "SAV2002", "SAV3045", "CORP0001"};

        for (String account : accounts) {
            byte[] keyBytes = account.getBytes();
            int partition = Math.abs(Utils.murmur2(keyBytes)) % numPartitions;
            System.out.println("  " + account + " -> partition " + partition);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
  SAV2001  -> partition 7
  SAV2002  -> partition 4
  SAV3045  -> partition 11
  CORP0001 -> partition 2
```

### How do you implement a custom Partitioner?

```java
package com.bank.partitioner;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.Partitioner;
import org.apache.kafka.common.Cluster;
import org.apache.kafka.common.utils.Utils;
import java.util.Map;

public class BankTierPartitioner implements Partitioner {
    private static final int PREMIUM_PARTITION_COUNT = 2;

    @Override
    public int partition(String topic, Object key, byte[] keyBytes,
                         Object value, byte[] valueBytes, Cluster cluster) {
        int numPartitions = cluster.partitionCountForTopic(topic);
        if (keyBytes == null) {
            return Math.abs(Utils.murmur2(new byte[0])) % numPartitions;
        }
        String keyStr = key.toString();
        if (keyStr.startsWith("PREMIUM-")) {
            return Math.abs(Utils.murmur2(keyBytes)) % PREMIUM_PARTITION_COUNT;
        }
        return PREMIUM_PARTITION_COUNT +
            Math.abs(Utils.murmur2(keyBytes)) % (numPartitions - PREMIUM_PARTITION_COUNT);
    }

    @Override public void close() { }
    @Override public void configure(Map<String, ?> configs) { }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("BankTierPartitioner:");
        System.out.println("  PREMIUM-SAV2001 -> partitions 0-1 (dedicated)");
        System.out.println("  SAV2001         -> partitions 2-11 (regular pool)");
    }
}
```

```text
BankTierPartitioner:
  PREMIUM-SAV2001 -> partitions 0-1 (dedicated)
  SAV2001         -> partitions 2-11 (regular pool)
```

### How do the strategies compare? (comparison table)

| Strategy | Ordering | Distribution | Use case |
|---|---|---|---|
| No key (sticky) | None between records | Even across all partitions | Independent events like audit logs |
| Hash-of-key | Per-key ordering | Even if key has high cardinality | Account-keyed banking events |
| Custom partitioner | Business-defined | Business-defined | Premium account routing |
| Explicit partition | Per-partition ordering | Manual | Rarely used, testing |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class PartitioningMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: using low-cardinality key for ordering
        System.out.println("WRONG: key = \"ACTIVE\" or \"INACTIVE\"");
        System.out.println("  Only 2 partitions used out of 12 -- massive skew");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: use high-cardinality key
        System.out.println("CORRECT: key = accountId (millions of values)");
        System.out.println("  Even spread across all 12 partitions");
        System.out.println();

        // WRONG: expecting global ordering across partitions
        System.out.println("WRONG: expecting transfer A before transfer B");
        System.out.println("  across partitions -- Kafka does NOT guarantee this");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: global ordering requires single partition (kills parallelism)
        System.out.println("CORRECT: for global order, use 1 partition (not recommended)");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: key = "ACTIVE" or "INACTIVE"
  Only 2 partitions used out of 12 -- massive skew

CORRECT: key = accountId (millions of values)
  Even spread across all 12 partitions

WRONG: expecting transfer A before transfer B
  across partitions -- Kafka does NOT guarantee this

CORRECT: for global order, use 1 partition (not recommended)
```

### What happens when you change the partition count?

Changing the partition count rehashes keys. A key that previously mapped to partition 3 might now map to partition 7. Records for that key produced before and after the change are on different partitions, so ordering across the boundary is not preserved.

```java
package com.bank.partition;

import org.apache.kafka.common.utils.Utils;

public class PartitionCountChange {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String key = "SAV2001";
        byte[] keyBytes = key.getBytes();
        int hash = Math.abs(Utils.murmur2(keyBytes));

        int partitionBefore = hash % 6;
        int partitionAfter = hash % 12;

        System.out.println("Key: " + key);
        System.out.println("  With 6 partitions  : partition " + partitionBefore);
        System.out.println("  With 12 partitions : partition " + partitionAfter);
        System.out.println("  Key moved! Ordering across the change is broken.");
    }
}
```

```text
Key: SAV2001
  With 6 partitions  : partition 1
  With 12 partitions : partition 7
  Key moved! Ordering across the change is broken.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Ordering is always only within a partition, never across partitions. Keying for ordering and keeping load even are in tension, so pick the key that satisfies the ordering requirement first, then address skew. A custom partitioner is powerful but becomes production code that must be tested and maintained. The sticky partitioner is preferred over the old round-robin for keyless records because it produces fuller batches and better throughput.
